# Roadmap sync after changes

When you **implement or materially change** product-facing behavior, APIs, data model, worker pipeline, ingestion, extraction, comparison, review, or dev stack conventions that the roadmaps track, **update the roadmaps in the same change** (same PR / same session) **if the “what’s built” story changed**.

## Files to keep in sync

| File | What to maintain |
|------|-------------------|
| [`product_roadmap.md`](product_roadmap.md) | **Implementation status** near the top: “Built so far”, “Still to build”, “Recommended next focus” — product-level language. Bump the section date when you edit it. |
| [`eng_roadmap.md`](eng_roadmap.md) | **§0.0 Implementation status** (table + “Not started” + “Suggested resume here order”), and **§0.1** step rows when a numbered step (1a–5e, 2a, P1, etc.) ships or meaningfully changes. Bump **Last updated** when you edit §0.0 / §0.1. |

Product = *what* and *why*; engineering = *what exists in repo* and *step IDs*. They should not contradict each other after your edit.

## When an update is **required**

- New or removed **user-visible** capability, **API route**, **DB table/column**, **worker stage**, **frontend flow**, or **dev script** that the roadmaps describe or imply.
- A roadmap row said “not started” / “TBD” / “thin” and your work **closes that gap**.
- **Milestone numbering** (1h, 2a, …) is now inaccurate.

## When you may **skip** roadmap edits

- Typo-only, comment-only, or **purely internal** refactors with **no** change to shipped behavior, contracts, or roadmap-tracked scope.
- Dependency version bumps with **no** feature impact described in the roadmaps.

If unsure, prefer a **one-line** roadmap tweak (e.g. “Last updated” + a single bullet) over leaving stale text.

## Style

- **Complete sentences**; avoid vague “updated stuff”.
- Prefer **concrete** references: file paths, endpoint paths, migration IDs, step labels (`2b`, `1h`).
- Do **not** expand roadmap scope into future design docs; only reflect **what changed** and **what is next** when it shifted.
